        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best splitting maul?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people the Fiskars Pro IsoCore 8 lb Splitting Maul is the best splitting maul: an 8 lb forged head with a riveted, shock-absorbing 36-inch handle that resists overstrike damage. If you prefer hickory and a made-in-USA tool, the Council Tool 7 lb PR70M is the best traditional option, and the Wilton B.A.S.H is the toughest handle for beginners.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a splitting maul and a splitting axe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A splitting maul has a heavier head, usually 6 to 8 lb, with a broad wedge and a flat poll, and it splits by mass. A splitting axe is lighter, around 3 to 5 lb, with a sharper edge, and splits by speed. Mauls handle big, knotty rounds; splitting axes are quicker on medium, straight-grained seasoned wood.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What weight splitting maul should I get?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most home splitters should choose a 6 to 8 lb maul. A 6 lb head suits smaller users, beginners and long sessions; 7 lb is a good middle ground for mixed hardwood; 8 lb is for large, knotty or frozen rounds and experienced splitters. Accuracy matters more than mass, so choose a weight you can place repeatedly without tiring.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it easier to split wood when it is frozen?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. Frozen wood is more brittle, and many rounds pop apart with less effort than in summer, which is why plenty of people do their splitting in winter. The trade-off is footing: clear snow and ice around the chopping block down to solid ground, or use sand or a mat, before swinging a heavy maul.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you hit a splitting wedge with a maul?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the maul's poll is designed for it. Some mauls, like the Husqvarna 32-inch maul, have a hardened striking face meant for driving wedges. Many splitting axes and hard-edged premium tools should not be used on steel wedges, because the poll can mushroom or chip. When in doubt, check the listing or use a sledgehammer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a splitting maul handle be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most full-size mauls use 32 to 36 inch handles. A 36-inch handle gives more leverage and keeps misses farther from your feet, which suits taller users. A 32-inch handle is easier to control. The right length lets the head land in the middle of the round with your arms extended and your back reasonably straight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I sharpen a splitting maul?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but it does not need a razor edge. A clean, moderately sharp edge bites into end grain and starts the split; a very thin edge chips on knots. Touch it up with a mill file after each session, keeping the original angle, and avoid overheating it on a fast grinder, which can soften the steel.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
